+	text =3D phoneRegex.ReplaceAllString(text, "[PHONE_REDACTED]")

The phone number regex pattern will incorrectly match SSN patterns since =
both use the XXX-XX-XXXX format. The phoneRegex (XXX-XXX-XXXX) is applied=
 after ssnRegex (XXX-XX-XXXX), but if you have a number like "123-456-789=
0", it could be a phone number, whereas "123-45-6789" is an SSN. However,=
 the issue is that both patterns will match strings with hyphens in simil=
ar positions. Consider making the patterns more specific or checking for =
word boundaries more carefully to avoid false positives.

> +	// Mask dates of birth (DOB)
+	dobRegex :=3D regexp.MustCompile(`\b\d{1,2}[/-]\d{1,2}[/-]\d{2,4}\b`)

The date of birth regex pattern is overly broad and will match any date-l=
ike string, not just dates of birth. The pattern `\b\d{1,2}[/-]\d{1,2}[/-=
]\d{2,4}\b` will match dates in messages that may not be DOBs (e.g., orde=
r dates, timestamps, scheduling dates). This creates false positives and =
may unnecessarily redact legitimate non-PHI dates. Consider either using =
a more contextual approach or documenting that this pattern may be overly=
 aggressive.
```suggestion
	// Mask dates of birth (DOB) using contextual patterns to avoid masking =
all dates
	dobRegex :=3D regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)\b(?:dob|d\.o\.b\.|date of birth)\=
s*[:\-]?\s*\d{1,2}[/-]\d{1,2}[/-]\d{2,4}\b`)
```

